This is a great feature. It allows the checker to easily download the batch data, verify it offline, and then return to the system to approve or reject.
Since you already process Excel files for bulk uploads, you already have the Apache POI library available in your backend.
Here is the complete implementation for both the Backend API and the Frontend React updates.
### 1. BACKEND: Add the Service Method
Open your **JournalRequestService.java** interface and add this method signature:
```java
byte[] generateBatchExcel(String batchId);

```
Then, open your **JournalRequestServiceImpl.java** and add the implementation. This method fetches all the rows for the batchId and dynamically builds an Excel file in memory:
```java
    @Override
    public byte[] generateBatchExcel(String batchId) {
        // Fetch all rows belonging to this batch
        List<JournalRequest> requests = journalRequestRepository.findByBatchId(batchId);
        
        if (requests == null || requests.isEmpty()) {
            throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("No records found for batch: " + batchId);
        }

        // Use Apache POI to create the Excel file
        try (org.apache.poi.xssf.usermodel.XSSFWorkbook workbook = new org.apache.poi.xssf.usermodel.XSSFWorkbook();
             java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream out = new java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream()) {

            org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Sheet sheet = workbook.createSheet("Batch Details");

            // 1. Create Header Row
            org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Row headerRow = sheet.createRow(0);
            String[] headers = {"Journal ID", "Branch", "Currency", "CGL", "Amount", "Type", "Remarks", "Product", "Date"};
            
            for (int i = 0; i < headers.length; i++) {
                org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Cell cell = headerRow.createCell(i);
                cell.setCellValue(headers[i]);
            }

            // 2. Populate Data Rows
            int rowIdx = 1;
            for (JournalRequest req : requests) {
                org.apache.poi.ss.usermodel.Row row = sheet.createRow(rowIdx++);
                
                row.createCell(0).setCellValue(req.getJournalId() != null ? req.getJournalId() : "");
                row.createCell(1).setCellValue(req.getBranchCode() != null ? req.getBranchCode() : "");
                row.createCell(2).setCellValue(req.getCurrency() != null ? req.getCurrency() : "");
                row.createCell(3).setCellValue(req.getCgl() != null ? req.getCgl() : "");

                BigDecimal amount = req.getAmount();
                if (amount != null) {
                    row.createCell(4).setCellValue(amount.abs().doubleValue());
                    row.createCell(5).setCellValue(amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0 ? "Credit" : "Debit");
                } else {
                    row.createCell(4).setCellValue(0);
                    row.createCell(5).setCellValue("");
                }

                row.createCell(6).setCellValue(req.getNarration() != null ? req.getNarration() : "");
                row.createCell(7).setCellValue(req.getProductCode() != null ? req.getProductCode() : "");
                row.createCell(8).setCellValue(req.getCsvDate() != null ? req.getCsvDate().toString() : "");
            }

            workbook.write(out);
            return out.toByteArray();

        } catch (java.io.IOException e) {
            log.error("EXCEL_GEN_ERROR | Batch: {} | Error: {}", batchId, e.getMessage());
            throw new CustomExceptions.DatabaseOperationException("Failed to generate Excel file", e);
        }
    }

```
### 2. BACKEND: Add the Controller Endpoint
Open your **JournalRequestController.java** and add this API endpoint to serve the generated byte array as a downloadable .xlsx file.
```java
    @GetMapping("/download-batch/{batchId}")
    public org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity<byte[]> downloadBatchExcel(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        byte[] excelData = journalRequestService.generateBatchExcel(batchId);

        org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders headers = new org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders();
        headers.setContentType(org.springframework.http.MediaType.parseMediaType("application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet"));
        headers.setContentDispositionFormData("attachment", "Batch_" + batchId + ".xlsx");
        headers.setCacheControl("must-revalidate, post-check=0, pre-check=0");

        return new org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity<>(excelData, headers, org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.OK);
    }

```
### 3. FRONTEND: Add the Download Logic (JournalAuthorization.js)
At the top of the file, make sure you import the download icon:
```javascript
import { FileDownload as FileDownloadIcon } from "@mui/icons-material";

```
Next, add this function to handle the API call and trigger the file download. You can place this function anywhere near your other handle... functions (like right below handleCloseViewModal):
```javascript
  const handleDownloadExcel = async () => {
    if (!viewingBatchId) return;
    
    try {
      showSnackBar("Downloading Excel...", "info");
      // Use 'blob' as the response type!
      const response = await callApi(
        `/JS/journals/download-batch/${viewingBatchId}`, 
        null, 
        "GET", 
        "blob", 
        null, 
        {}, 
        false
      );
      
      const blobData = response.data || response;
      const blob = new Blob([blobData], { type: "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet" });
      const url = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      
      const link = document.createElement("a");
      link.href = url;
      link.setAttribute("download", `Batch_${viewingBatchId}.xlsx`);
      document.body.appendChild(link);
      link.click();
      link.remove();
      window.URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
      
      showSnackBar("Download complete.", "success");
    } catch (e) {
      showSnackBar("Failed to download Excel file.", "error");
    }
  };

```
